#:script

###
# Kobo partners
**Kobo Partners** are a language feature that provides an alternative to traditional inheritance by allowing classes to collaborate through 
explicitly defined relationships. Rather than inheriting behavior from a parent class, a collaborator attaches partner classes to extend its 
capabilities through composition. Each partnership creates a specialized relationship in which the partner can access members shared by the 
collaborator, while maintaining clear boundaries around visibility and ownership. Kobo supports both independently instantiable partner types 
and collaboration-specific nested partner types, whose instances and members remain confined to their associated collaboration. This approach 
promotes modularity, encapsulation, and code reuse without the tight coupling and rigid hierarchies of traditional inheritance.

In the sample below we have a class that has been declared such that it can participate in partnership with any class that wants to serve as
the orchestrator of the partnership
###

[public, partner] class:Wheel
{
    function:void Turn(int rate)
    {
        Print("Turning wheel at ${rate}")
    }
}

###
Partnering with this class, as defined above, would make little sense since no internal fields are externally exposed.  To expose capability to the 
orchestrator of the partnership use the shared keyword.  ```shared``` makes the property, field, or function accesible to the orchestrator **alone**.  Other
partner objects will still not have any access to it
###

[public, partner] class:Engine
{
    property:int Speed

    [shared]
    function:void Drive(int i)
       requires i > 0    
    {
        Speed = i
        Print("Driving")
    }

    function:void Stop()
    {
        Print("Stopping")
    }

}

###
You can orchestrate a partnership between declared partner classes using this ```partners``` keyword as part of class declaration
###

[public] class:Car partners Wheel, Engine
{
    
    [public] function:void DriveCar()
    {
        Drive(65)
        
    }
}

###
In this sample the car object can call DriveCar but has not visibility to call ```Drive```
###
var car = new Car()
car.DriveCar()
//car.Drive() //throws an error

###
You can still instantiate partner classes by themselved (assuming they are instantiable)
###
var:Wheel wheel = new Wheel()
//wheel.Turn() private so can't be accessed


var engine = new Engine();
//engine.Drive(4) //still inaccesible, sharing is only to the orchestrator in the partnership